2
00:00:02,123 --> 00:00:04,330
This curve, f of x equals x cubed

3
00:00:04,391 --> 00:00:07,159
minus three x, has a local maximum at

4
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:08,083
minus one, two.

5
00:00:08,966 --> 00:00:11,313
Now we only look from x equals minus

6
00:00:11,375 --> 00:00:14,084
two to x equals three, with both ends

7
00:00:14,145 --> 00:00:14,525
included.

8
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:17,638
Near minus one, nothing is higher than two.

9
00:00:18,223 --> 00:00:20,430
But follow the curve to the right-hand end.

10
00:00:21,134 --> 00:00:23,490
At x equals three, f is eighteen.

11
00:00:24,055 --> 00:00:26,803
So a local maximum isn't always the largest

12
00:00:26,864 --> 00:00:27,204
value.

13
00:00:27,947 --> 00:00:29,912
How do we find the largest and smallest

14
00:00:29,973 --> 00:00:32,077
values on a whole interval, and make sure

15
00:00:32,118 --> 00:00:32,899
we haven't missed one?

16
00:00:33,529 --> 00:00:36,059
The largest value over the whole domain is

17
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:37,686
called the global maximum.

18
00:00:38,410 --> 00:00:38,769
A point

19
00:00:39,371 --> 00:00:41,851
a, f of a, is a global maximum

20
00:00:42,172 --> 00:00:43,852
if f of x is less than or

21
00:00:43,993 --> 00:00:46,273
equal to f of a, for every x

22
00:00:46,455 --> 00:00:47,014
in the domain.

23
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:50,052
Picture a horizontal line at that height.

24
00:00:50,656 --> 00:00:52,623
For a global maximum, no part of the

25
00:00:52,664 --> 00:00:53,607
graph pokes above it.

26
00:00:54,310 --> 00:00:56,317
Put the line at two, the local maximum,

27
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:58,827
and the curve pokes through near the right-hand

28
00:00:58,928 --> 00:00:59,068
end.

29
00:00:59,711 --> 00:01:01,797
Put it at eighteen, and the whole graph

30
00:01:01,938 --> 00:01:03,201
sits on or below it.

31
00:01:03,945 --> 00:01:06,012
A global minimum is the same idea with

32
00:01:06,053 --> 00:01:06,534
a floor.

33
00:01:07,037 --> 00:01:08,644
No part of the graph dips below it.

34
00:01:09,289 --> 00:01:10,836
So where can the highest point be?

35
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:14,065
Suppose it's inside the interval, where the curve

36
00:01:14,106 --> 00:01:14,507
is smooth.

37
00:01:15,170 --> 00:01:17,104
Then its tangent must be flat.

38
00:01:17,991 --> 00:01:20,216
If the tangent sloped either way, you could

39
00:01:20,337 --> 00:01:22,200
step a little uphill and find a higher

40
00:01:22,261 --> 00:01:22,501
point.

41
00:01:23,185 --> 00:01:25,561
So inside, look at stationary points.

42
00:01:26,326 --> 00:01:28,414
But at an end, the curve just stops.

43
00:01:29,098 --> 00:01:30,905
It doesn't need to be flat, because the

44
00:01:31,107 --> 00:01:32,875
uphill direction leaves the domain.

45
00:01:33,599 --> 00:01:35,884
And at a sharp corner, there's no single

46
00:01:35,965 --> 00:01:39,553
tangent, so f prime doesn't exist there, and

47
00:01:39,614 --> 00:01:41,939
solving f prime equals zero can't find it.

48
00:01:42,822 --> 00:01:44,364
On a closed interval where the curve is

49
00:01:44,505 --> 00:01:48,190
unbroken, the highest and lowest values always exist,

50
00:01:48,651 --> 00:01:50,012
and each one is at one of these

51
00:01:50,073 --> 00:01:50,533
candidates.

52
00:01:51,276 --> 00:01:53,603
If the curve breaks somewhere, examine that point

53
00:01:53,645 --> 00:01:53,844
too.

54
00:01:54,543 --> 00:01:55,918
That gives a short method.

55
00:01:56,482 --> 00:01:57,264
List the candidates:

56
00:01:57,825 --> 00:02:01,254
stationary points inside the interval, any interior points

57
00:02:01,314 --> 00:02:02,778
where f prime doesn't exist,

58
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:04,383
and both endpoints.

59
00:02:05,005 --> 00:02:07,321
Then work out f at every one, and

60
00:02:07,362 --> 00:02:07,703
compare.

61
00:02:08,648 --> 00:02:11,438
Here, f prime of x is three, times

62
00:02:11,619 --> 00:02:13,907
x minus one, times x plus one.

63
00:02:14,389 --> 00:02:16,961
So the stationary points are minus one and

64
00:02:17,163 --> 00:02:18,308
one, both inside.

65
00:02:19,072 --> 00:02:21,040
The endpoints are minus two and three,

66
00:02:21,563 --> 00:02:23,310
and f prime exists everywhere.

67
00:02:24,213 --> 00:02:27,300
The values are minus two, two, minus two

68
00:02:27,321 --> 00:02:28,442
and eighteen.

69
00:02:29,085 --> 00:02:31,834
The largest is eighteen, so the global maximum

70
00:02:31,956 --> 00:02:34,103
is eighteen, at x equals three.

71
00:02:34,846 --> 00:02:37,153
The smallest is minus two, and it happens

72
00:02:37,234 --> 00:02:37,594
twice,

73
00:02:38,117 --> 00:02:39,380
at x equals minus two

74
00:02:39,883 --> 00:02:40,945
and x equals one.

75
00:02:41,829 --> 00:02:43,816
Notice we never had to classify anything.

76
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:45,596
Comparing the values settles it.

77
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:47,243
Now a worked example.

78
00:02:47,829 --> 00:02:50,236
Find the global maximum and minimum of f

79
00:02:50,257 --> 00:02:52,663
of x equals x plus four over x,

80
00:02:53,025 --> 00:02:54,609
for x from a half to four.

81
00:02:55,353 --> 00:02:55,632
First,

82
00:02:56,275 --> 00:02:58,520
f prime of x is one minus four

83
00:02:58,682 --> 00:02:59,463
over x squared.

84
00:03:00,146 --> 00:03:02,374
Setting that to zero gives x squared equals

85
00:03:02,495 --> 00:03:05,386
four, so x is two or minus two.

86
00:03:06,091 --> 00:03:08,024
Minus two isn't in the interval, so reject

87
00:03:08,045 --> 00:03:08,144
it.

88
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:11,531
And f isn't defined at zero, but zero

89
00:03:11,652 --> 00:03:12,772
